{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CfpoQVTaMnFrxmmBHcFyq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Happy to park the code. Let me push on the framing first, because I think the binary is the weak part of the question.\n\n**Most items are both.** \"Done\" and \"needs a human call\" aren't alternatives — the usual case is that the work is complete *and* a judgment call rode along inside it. If we treat them as exclusive, we get the two failure modes: an agent that blocks on everything (useless) or one that silently resolves everything and reports a clean \"done ✅\" (dangerous, because the report carries no information about residual risk). The better output is: finished thing + the list of decisions I made that you might want to overturn. Blocking with nothing delivered should be rare — reserved for cases where guessing wrong makes the work actively harmful or worthless, not merely wrong.\n\n**Two kinds of ambiguity, and only one is worth your time.** Before escalating anything, the test is: *is there evidence that would settle this?* \"What does this codebase do with non-string input?\" is a lookup — I should go read `src/str.js` and find that `trim` just lets `.trim()` throw, and follow precedent. Asking you that is pure overhead. \"Should `café` become `cafe` or `caf`?\" is a different animal: no amount of reading the repo settles it, because it's a question about who the users are. That's a preference, and preferences are yours. So: never ask what I can observe; don't guess at what only you can decide.\n\n**What makes a decision escalation-worthy** is roughly reversibility × blast radius × *detectability* — and I think detectability is the underrated one. A wrong guess that blows up loudly is cheap; you find out in an hour. The `café` guess is worth surfacing less because it's hard to undo and more because it's **silently** wrong — if the test corpus is all English, ASCII-only slugify looks perfect forever and starts mangling names only when a real user shows up. Decisions whose errors hide are worth interrupting you over even when they're individually small.\n\n**The uncomfortable one: green tests are a weaker signal than they sound**, specifically when I wrote both the tests and the code. 8/8 passing then proves the implementation is consistent with my assumptions — it cannot validate the assumptions, because they're baked into both halves. I could ship something that destroys every non-English string and have a perfectly green suite. That's the classic oracle problem, and it means \"all tests pass\" is a decent *necessary* condition for done and a bad sufficient one. The useful companion question is the inverse of coverage: **what inputs did I never try?** My table maps each test to a clause of the issue; the risk lives in the rows that aren't there.\n\n**And a caveat about me specifically.** I'm not reliably calibrated about my own uncertainty, and I'll say \"done\" in the same confident register whether I verified something or inferred it. So I'd put weight on checks that don't depend on my introspection — \"done\" has to cite observed output rather than expected output (I ran `npm test` and pasted the 1 pass / 8 fail counts, rather than asserting the tests fail), small items so the diff is reviewable, assumptions stated explicitly enough to be contradicted. Process robust to my miscalibration beats asking me to self-report confidence.\n\nOne thing I'd want your read on: a lot of late-discovered ambiguity is really an upstream symptom. ISSUE.md never says what \"alphanumeric\" means for non-ASCII, and that gap was visible before a line was written. Is the fix mostly better-specified issues, or do you want the ambiguity surfaced at the end as a standing part of how I report? They trade off — front-loading costs you time on items where the ambiguity would never have mattered."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10325,"cache_read_input_tokens":12800,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":10325},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"14d8e302-bc69-4be5-afc4-d9e613b98447","uuid":"e7ebe16a-d3ee-488d-b4e9-13d1e1b42a8d","timestamp":"2026-10-08T12:52:57.300Z","request_id":"req_011CfpoQVADQTzMZBfs1pzxm"}
